.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH RTRASH 1 "2026-07-06" "rtrash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rtrash \- fast freedesktop.org trash tool with an rm-compatible interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rtrash
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]...
.PP
.B rtrash
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.PP
Multi-call names (symlinks to the same binary):
.BR trash-put ,
.BR trash ,
.BR trash-empty ,
.BR trash-list ,
.BR trash-restore ,
.BR trash-rm ,
and optionally
.B rm
(put into trash).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rtrash
implements the freedesktop.org Trash specification on Linux: home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR) and per-volume trash directories, with atomic
.B .trashinfo
files. It provides an rm-compatible put path and a trash-cli-style suite
(list, restore, empty, selective permanent delete).
.PP
Invoked as
.B rtrash
with a known subcommand, that command runs. Invoked as
.BR rtrash
with no subcommand (or as
.BR rm ,
.BR trash ,
or
.BR trash-put ),
arguments are treated as
.B put
(move into the trash). The multi-call name
.B trash-rm
is permanent delete of matching
.I trash entries
, not the same as multi-call
.B rm
(which puts).
.PP
File names need not be UTF-8. An argument or a trashed name with other
bytes keeps them through every command. Output shows each such byte as
the Unicode replacement character.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal, names and paths in text output show
each control character (C0, DEL, and C1) and each bidirectional override
as
.BR ? ,
as
.BR ls (1)
does. A name or a
.B Path=
line in a shared trash can't move the cursor, recolor the screen, or fake
extra rows that way. A pipe, a file, and
.B \-\-json
still get the raw name. Errors about a trashed item on standard error
follow the same rule when standard error is a terminal. The browsers
drop control characters when they draw.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
When
.B rtrash
is invoked with no subcommand yet recognized:
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS put
.B rtrash put
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.PP
Also:
.BR trash-put ,
.BR trash ,
.BR rm
(multi-call), or bare
.B rtrash
with paths/flags.
.PP
Move
.I FILE
s into the trash. Accepts common
.BR rm (1)
flags with the same interactive/force semantics; nothing is unlinked
permanently.
.PP
A trash on another file system gets a copy, and the original goes only
once the copy is whole. The copy keeps holes in sparse files, mode, owner
where allowed, times, extended attributes, named pipes, and hard links
inside the tree. Within one btrfs file system it clones instead.
A tree with a directory this user can't empty is refused before anything
moves. Sockets can't be copied, so trashing one there fails. Restore
follows the same rules.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent files and arguments, never prompt.
.TP
.B \-i
Prompt before every removal.
.TP
.B \-I
Prompt once before removing more than three files, or when removing
recursively.
.TP
.BI \-\-interactive [=WHEN]
Prompt according to
.IR WHEN :
.BR never ,
.BR once
(\-I), or
.BR always
(\-i); without
.IR WHEN ,
prompt always.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Remove directories and their contents (into the trash).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Remove empty directories.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Explain what is being done.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
Accepted for rm compatibility (no-op: entries are moved whole, never
traversed like
.BR "rm \-x" ).
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve\-root [ =all ]
Do not remove
.RB \(oq / \(cq ,
your home directory, or a directory on another device than its parent
(a mount point; renaming fails there, and a copy would drag the whole
file system into the trash).
The default;
.B =all
spells the same thing in GNU terms.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
Do not treat those specially.
.PP
Whatever the flags, put refuses a path that is, or contains, the trash
directory it would move into (a directory cannot move inside itself),
and an item already inside that trash, which would lose its record
there; to delete trashed items, use
.B rtrash rm
or
.BR "rtrash empty" .
.PP
Put also skips paths on the safe-rm list: safe-rm's built-in system
directories (such as
.BR /usr ,
.BR /etc ,
.BR /home )
plus every path or shell pattern, one per line, in
.BR /etc/safe\-rm.conf ,
.BR /usr/local/etc/safe\-rm.conf ,
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/safe\-rm
and
.BR ~/.safe\-rm .
No flag overrides that list.
Before it trashes a directory with a git repository at its top that
has uncommitted, unpushed or unreadable work, put prints a warning;
.B rtrash undo
brings the run back.
.TP
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Put into this FreeDesktop trash root (creates
.B files/
and
.B info/
if needed). Scripts and tests use this instead of XDG discovery.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Always use the home trash (skip volume trash selection).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Never open the put browser; require
.I FILE
operands even on a TTY.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-space\-check
Copy across devices even when the copy would leave the trash's file
system with under 5% of its bytes or inodes free, or under
.B RTRASH_MIN_FREE
bytes.
Without it, such a put fails before it copies anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-in\-use= WHEN
What to do when a live process holds an item, as
.B rtrash who
reports it: an open descriptor or memory map on it or below it, or a
working, root or executable path there.
.B warn
(the default) trashes it and names the processes on stderr,
.B refuse
leaves it and counts it as failed, and
.B ignore
skips the process scan.
It overrides
.B RTRASH_IN_USE
and
.BR policy.in_use .
The interactive browsers do not scan.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object on stdout:
\fItrashed\fR (one entry per moved item with \fIoriginal\fR, \fIname\fR, \fItrash_dir\fR)
and \fIfailed\fR (operands that could not be trashed).
Implies
.B \-\-plain
and replaces
.B \-v
output.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS empty
.B rtrash empty
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIDAYS\fR]
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-empty .
.PP
Purge trashed items. With
.IR DAYS ,
only items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
An item whose
.B DeletionDate
is missing or unreadable has no known age, so
.I DAYS
keeps it, as trash-cli does; a full
.B empty
removes it.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be removed without removing anything; also prints
approximate reclaimable disk space.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each removed item.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Accepted for trash-cli compatibility (emptying never prompts).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Empty only this trash directory (repeatable); default is the home trash
plus every mounted volume.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash discovery).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Same as positional
.I DAYS
(list/status flag style).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-size =SIZE
Remove items until the trash directories in scope, taken together, fit in
.I SIZE
(bytes, or with a K, M, G or T suffix in powers of 1024).
With
.IR DAYS ,
the age cut runs first.
.TP
.BR \-\-prune =oldest|largest
Which items
.B \-\-max\-size
removes first (default oldest).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON summary on stdout
(\fIdry_run\fR, \fIremoved\fR, \fIbytes\fR, \fIdays\fR, \fImax_size\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Never open the empty browser on a TTY.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
A directory the user owns but cannot list or change (mode 0311, say)
gets owner
.B rwx
back and then goes; nothing another account owns is changed.
An item that still cannot go stays, with its
.BR .trashinfo .
The rest still goes, and
.B empty
exits 1 after listing each item left and the reason.
.SS list
.B rtrash list
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-list .
.PP
List trashed items as
.RB \(oq "DELETION-DATE ORIGINAL-PATH" \(cq,
oldest first.
A record named
.IR .trashinfo ,
.I ..trashinfo
or
.I ...trashinfo
names no item, since its payload in
.I files/
would be
.I files/
itself or the whole trash; no command lists it or acts on it, and a full
.B empty
clears it.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
List only this trash directory (repeatable); default is the home trash
plus every mounted volume.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-users
List the trash of every account this user can read, with an owner column
after the date (and an
.B owner
field in JSON).
It reads each home trash at the default
.IR ~/.local/share/Trash ,
since another account's
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unknown, plus every
.I .Trash/$uid
and
.I .Trash\-$uid
on mounted volumes.
Root sees them all.
Nothing changes on disk; to restore or remove another account's items,
root pins that trash with
.BR \-\-trash\-dir .
Takes no
.B \-\-home\-only
or
.BR \-\-trash\-dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago (same cutoff model as
.BR "empty DAYS" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.BI \-\-larger\-than =SIZE
Only items taking more than
.I SIZE
on disk, payload plus
.IR .trashinfo ,
the size
.B list \-\-size
shows.
.I SIZE
reads as in
.BR "empty \-\-max\-size" :
500M, 1.5G, 64k.
Payloads with no
.I .trashinfo
have a size, so this filter keeps them in.
.TP
.BI \-\-smaller\-than =SIZE
Only items taking less than
.I SIZE
on disk.
.TP
.B \-\-size
Add a column with each item's disk usage: the payload plus its
.IR .trashinfo ,
as
.B status
counts it.
Directory sizes come from
.I directorysizes
when valid, and walked sizes go back into it.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort= KEY
Order rows by
.B date
(the default),
.B size
(largest first; implies
.BR \-\-size ),
.B name
(original file name) or
.B path
(original path).
Ties keep the date order.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the order.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON array of objects
(\fIdeletion_date\fR, \fIoriginal\fR, \fIname\fR, \fItrash_dir\fR,
\fIbatch\fR, and \fIsize\fR in bytes with
.BR \-\-size ).
The batch holds the put run id for items rtrash trashed, else null.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Orphans (payloads in
.B files/
with no
.BR .trashinfo )
come last, dated
.B ????-??-??
and shown by their path inside the trash, with
.B \(dqorphan\(dq:true
in JSON.
An age filter leaves them out.
.SS status
.B rtrash status
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Summarize discovered trash: item count and approximate reclaimable size
(per root and total). Uses the same disk-usage walk as
.BR "empty \-\-dry\-run" .
A line for each trash then gives the free bytes and free inodes of its
file system, and a warning goes to stderr when either is under 10%.
The JSON roots carry them as
.BR fs_bytes ", " fs_free_bytes ", " fs_inodes ", " fs_free_inodes
(null without an inode limit) and
.BR fs_low .
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only count items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only count items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.BI \-\-larger\-than =SIZE
Only count items taking more than
.I SIZE
on disk, payload plus
.IR .trashinfo ,
the size
.B list \-\-size
shows.
.I SIZE
reads as in
.BR "empty \-\-max\-size" :
500M, 1.5G, 64k.
Payloads with no
.I .trashinfo
have a size, so this filter keeps them in.
.TP
.BI \-\-smaller\-than =SIZE
Only count items taking less than
.I SIZE
on disk.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON object
(\fIroots\fR, \fItotal_items\fR, \fItotal_bytes\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Orphans count as items, noted as
.RB \(oq "(N without .trashinfo)" \(cq
per root and as
.B orphans
in JSON; an age filter leaves them out.
.PP
On Windows, status lists each drive's Recycle Bin: items, size, and the
cap from
.B MaxCapacity
in the registry with the share in use, then the total.
A drive set to skip the Recycle Bin says so.
A
.B Free on
line per drive then gives its free bytes from
.BR GetDiskFreeSpaceExW ,
with a warning under 10% free; Windows counts no inodes.
With an age or size filter it counts the passing items per drive
instead, without the caps.
.PP
On Windows,
.BR list ,
.BR status ,
.B rm
and
.B empty
take the same age filters as here, on the deletion time Windows
records, and all but
.B empty
take the size filters.
An item's size is the one its
.I $I
record holds, which for a folder is its total at deletion.
.SS du
.B rtrash du
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIPATH\fR]...
.br
.B rtrash du diff
[\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-apparent\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.I OLD
.RI [ NEW ]
.PP
.B du diff
compares two snapshots written with
.B \-o
(or ncdu exports, see
.BR \-f )
and lists the directories that grew or shrank most, marking those that
appeared (new) or left (gone).
Without
.IR NEW ,
it scans the roots of
.I OLD
again at the same depth.
To scan a directory named diff, write
.BR "rtrash du ./diff" .
.PP
Find what to delete. Walk each
.I PATH
(default the current directory) in parallel and list the largest
directories and files with their on-disk and apparent sizes and the age of
their newest modification. Symlinks are never followed, and a file with
more than one hard link counts once, in the sizes and in the file count,
so files plus directories match
.BR "du \-\-inodes \-s" ;
the total names the extra links. When one subdirectory accounts for
90% or more of a directory's size, only the subdirectory is listed.
The scan root itself appears only in the closing total.
A root that is a regenerable artefact (see
.BR \-\-artefacts )
has its kind named in the total, and its contents are listed, since
naming it asks to look inside.
.PP
Rows inside or containing a git repository carry a git state, found with
the
.B git
binary.
.B ignored
means an ignore rule matches the path;
.B clean
means its repository has nothing uncommitted;
.B dirty
means uncommitted changes, untracked files or a stash;
.B unpushed
means commits on a local branch that no remote has;
.B unknown
means git gave no answer.
A directory takes the riskiest state among its own repository and every
repository below it.
.B \-\-print0
leaves out dirty, unpushed and unknown rows.
Paths inside
.I .git
or a bare repository are never listed.
A bare repository is one row, and it reads
.B unpushed
when no remote has its commits.
.PP
The closing summary reports the trash on the scanned file systems.
Trashed files keep their space until
.B rtrash empty
removes them; preview with
.BR "rtrash empty \-n" ,
or keep recent items with
.BR "rtrash empty " \fIDAYS\fR.
A trash directory found during the walk is one
.B trash
row, and
.B \-\-print0
never prints it.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-top =\fIN\fR
List
.I N
directories and
.I N
files (default 20).
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
Skip directories on other file systems.
.TP
.B \-\-apparent
Rank by apparent size (file length) instead of allocated disk space.
As with coreutils
.BR du (1),
directories add to disk use but not to apparent size.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth =N
List only entries at most
.I N
levels below
.IR PATH .
Sizes still include everything below.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads =N
Walk with
.I N
threads. The default is one per CPU, or
.B RAYON_NUM_THREADS
when it is set. Without either, rtrash first times a few stats and opens
under the first
.IR PATH ;
when they average 10 microseconds or more, the metadata is coming off
the disk, and the walk uses three threads per CPU, up to 64. Cached, the
probe costs well under a millisecond and changes nothing. More threads
than CPUs can also help on a remote file system.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-dirs
List every directory level, including parents that one subdirectory
dominates.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort= KEY
Rank rows by
.IR KEY :
.B disk
(the default),
.B apparent
(the same as
.BR \-\-apparent ),
.B items
(files and directories below each directory, each hard-linked file once;
no file rows are listed, since every file holds one item), or
.B mtime
(newest modification first). Under
.BR mtime ,
a directory gives way to the subdirectory that contains its newest change,
so the rows name where the change happened. Snapshots do not record item
counts, so
.B \-\-file
takes every key but
.BR items .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-count
Add an ITEMS column. The JSON rows always carry
.BR items .
.TP
.B \-\-inodes
Rank by inodes, the same as
.BR \-\-sort=items :
files and directories, each hard-linked file once.
The total line and the JSON roots also give the inodes each PATH uses.
.TP
.B \-\-types
List file types instead of directories and files: disk use, apparent
size and file count for each extension, the text after the last dot,
with case ignored.
Names with no extension, including names that start with their only dot,
make one row, and an extension over 12 characters counts as none.
The first
.B \-\-top
types are listed and one line sums the rest; the totals stay.
Ranks by disk use,
.B \-\-apparent
or
.B \-\-sort=items
(file count).
JSON adds a
.B types
array of every type, each with
.BR type ,
.BR disk ,
.B apparent
and
.BR files .
Takes no
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-stacks ,
.BR \-\-print0 ,
.B \-\-pick
or
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B \-\-unique
Add a UNIQUE column: the bytes of each row in extents no other file
shares, roughly what deleting it would free.
On Linux the walk asks
.B FS_IOC_FIEMAP
for every file and counts the extents not flagged shared, which btrfs
and the XFS family set for reflinks and snapshots; other Linux file
systems share nothing, so the column matches DISK.
On macOS it reads the private size APFS keeps for clones.
Elsewhere, and for files it cannot open, the column matches DISK.
The totals add a unique figure, and a closing line gives the shared
extents once each and as the files reference them.
JSON rows and roots gain
.BR unique ,
and the report gains
.BR shared .
Mapping extents costs time, so the plain listing skips it, and even
then only files of at least 64 KiB on disk
.RB ( RTRASH_EXTENT_MIN )
are mapped, and only on btrfs, XFS, bcachefs, OCFS2 and APFS; any
other file counts as unique.
Not with
.BR \-f ", " \-o ", " \-\-stacks " or " \-i .
.TP
.B \-\-shared
Count each shared extent once in every row and total, so reflinked
copies and snapshots no longer add up to more than they take on disk.
On Linux the walk asks
.B FS_IOC_FIEMAP
for every file; btrfs and the XFS family flag the extents that
reflinks and snapshots share.
A row counts its files' own extents, plus the shared ones it points
at, each once.
An extent shared with files outside the row still counts in full in
that row, since the row uses it.
APFS reports no shared ranges, so on macOS, as on file systems that
share nothing, every file counts in full.
The DISK column and the ranking then show these sizes, and the
totals also give the size that counts each reference.
JSON roots gain
.BR disk_all .
It costs as much as
.B \-\-unique
and can go with it.
Not with
.BR \-f ", " \-o ", " \-\-stacks ", " \-i ", " \-\-types ", " \-\-ncdu " or " \-\-ncdu\-binary .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= PATTERN
Leave out entries that match
.I PATTERN
(repeatable). Patterns use gitignore syntax: one without a
.B /
matches a name at any depth, one with a
.B /
matches the path relative to
.I PATH
(an absolute path under
.I PATH
works too), a trailing
.B /
matches directories only,
.B *
and
.B ?
stop at
.BR / ,
and
.B **
spans directories.
Excluded entries add nothing to any size or count, and an excluded
directory is never opened. The total reports how many entries were left
out.
On Unix, matching works on bytes: a pattern, ignore file or
.B \-X
file holding bytes that are not UTF-8 matches names with the same bytes.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-exclude\-from=\fIFILE\fR
Read patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line, with
.B #
comments and
.B !
to re-include an entry an earlier pattern left out.
.B \-
reads standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-caches
Leave out directories below
.I PATH
that hold a
.I CACHEDIR.TAG
starting with the standard signature line.
.TP
.B \-\-gitignore
Leave out what git ignores: patterns from
.I .gitignore
files,
.I .git/info/exclude
and
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore ,
applied only inside git repositories. A nested repository starts afresh,
as in git. A
.B core.excludesFile
set elsewhere is not read.
.TP
.B \-\-artefacts
List only regenerable build artefacts and caches.
Each one is a single row, and nothing below it is listed on its own.
The tags are
.B cargo\-target
(a directory with
.I .rustc_info.json
or a
.I target
directory with
.IR CACHEDIR.TAG ),
.B node\-modules ,
.B python\-cache
(\fI__pycache__\fR, \fI.pytest_cache\fR, \fI.mypy_cache\fR, \fI.ruff_cache\fR, \fI.hypothesis\fR),
.B python\-env
(a directory with
.IR pyvenv.cfg ,
\fI.tox\fR, \fI.nox\fR),
.B cmake\-build
(a directory with
.IR CMakeCache.txt ),
.B gradle\-cache ,
.B js\-build\-cache
(\fI.next\fR, \fI.nuxt\fR, \fI.parcel\-cache\fR, \fI.turbo\fR, \fI.svelte\-kit\fR),
.B zig\-cache ,
.B pixi\-env ,
.B direnv ,
and
.B cache
for any other directory with a
.I CACHEDIR.TAG
file.
The same tags appear in the NOTE column without this flag.
.TP
.B \-\-dirs
Show the directory table alone.
.TP
.B \-\-files
Show the file table alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only list entries whose newest modification is more than
.I DAYS
days old. A directory uses the newest file anywhere below it.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only list entries modified within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.BI \-\-since =DATE
Only list entries modified at or after
.IR DATE :
.I YYYY\-MM\-DD
for local midnight, or
.I YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM
with optional
.I :SS
in local time.
.TP
.BI \-\-until =DATE
Only list entries whose newest modification is before
.IR DATE ,
in the same form.
With more than one of the age options, each limit applies.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Consider only the home trash for the trash summary and
.B trash
rows.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Consider only this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-git
Do not ask git about listed paths.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-dirty
With
.BR \-\-print0 ,
also print rows whose git state is dirty, unpushed or unknown.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit one JSON object
(\fIroots\fR, \fIdirs\fR, \fIfiles\fR, \fIunreadable\fR); each root has
\fIpath\fR, \fIdisk\fR, \fIapparent\fR, \fIfiles\fR, \fIlinks\fR (extra hard links),
\fIdirs\fR and \fIartefact\fR; each listed entry has
\fIpath\fR, \fItype\fR, \fIdisk\fR, \fIapparent\fR, \fImtime\fR, \fIartefact\fR
(a tag or null), \fIgit\fR and \fIgit_repo\fR (state and deciding repository, or null).
A scan stopped by Ctrl-C adds \fIpartial\fR: true.
The walk enters at most 4096 levels and paths of up to 32 KiB; a
directory past either counts with its own blocks but not its contents,
stderr says how many there were, and the object adds \fItoo_deep\fR.
Entries the walk could not read are counted in \fIunreadable\fR and
named in \fIunreadable_paths\fR (up to 100, each with \fIpath\fR and \fIerror\fR);
stderr names the first 10 and counts the rest.
.TP
.B \-\-print0
Print the listed paths separated by null bytes, for
.BR "xargs \-0 rtrash put \-rf \-\-" .
.TP
.B \-\-pick
Open the put browser on the listed rows (directories, then files).
Mark rows and press Enter to trash them after a confirmation; rows with
dirty, unpushed or unknown git state show
.B !
and the confirmation counts them.
Needs a terminal on stdin and the
.B tui
feature.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output =\fIFILE\fR
Write a snapshot of every directory total, down to
.BR \-\-max\-depth ,
to
.I FILE
.RB ( \- " for stdout)"
instead of listing.
Snapshots hold one record per line with percent-encoded paths, so grep and
text diff tools read them too.
.TP
.B \-\-devices
List every mounted file system instead of scanning: size, use, free space,
use percent (rounded up, as
.BR df (1)
rounds it), type, device and mount point.
As with
.BR df ,
pseudo file systems, file systems with no blocks and second mounts of one
device (bind mounts) are left out.
On Windows the fixed and removable drives are listed, with their labels.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
one object with a \fIdevices\fR array of \fImount\fR, \fIdevice\fR, \fItype\fR,
\fIsize\fR, \fIused\fR, \fIavailable\fR and \fIuse_percent\fR.
Takes no
.IR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-ncdu= FILE
Write an ncdu export of the one
.I PATH
to
.I FILE
(\fB\-\fR for stdout) instead of listing: \fBncdu \-f\fR \fIFILE\fR browses it, and
.B \-f
reads it back.
\fB\-x\fR and the filters apply; what they leave out is written by name,
marked excluded.
Entries that cannot be read are marked \fIread_error\fR, as are directories
past the walk's limits of 4096 levels and 32 KiB of path, written without
their contents.
The export holds every file, so it grows with the tree (about 80 bytes an
entry).
.TP
.BI \-\-ncdu\-binary= FILE
Like
.BR \-\-ncdu ,
but in the binary format of ncdu 2.6 and later
.RB ( "ncdu \-O" ):
compressed with zstd to about a fifth of the JSON export, and holding each
directory's cumulative sizes and item count, which ncdu reads instead of
adding up the tree.
Hard links count as ncdu counts them: once in every directory with a link
below it, with the part shared with links elsewhere recorded too.
The fast zstd level is the only one this build has, so the export is
larger than ncdu's own, by about a third.
A second thread compresses while the walk goes on, so the export takes
about as long as the JSON one.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file =\fIFILE\fR
List the largest directories from a snapshot instead of scanning.
\fIFILE\fR may also be an ncdu export (\fBncdu \-o\fR or \fBgdu \-o\fR, or the
binary export of ncdu 2.6 and later, \fBncdu \-O\fR, told
apart by its first bytes): its totals are counted as a scan counts them,
with each hard link counted once (when the export records inodes)
and excluded entries left out.
gdu exports carry no directory sizes or inodes, so their totals can be lower or higher.
An export written without \fBncdu \-e\fR has no modification times: AGE reads \-
and the JSON \fImtime\fR is null.
A binary export records no scan date, so du uses the file's modification
time instead.
A snapshot can be stale, so it cannot drive
.BR \-\-print0 ,
.BR \-\-pick " or " \-\-interactive .
.TP
.B \-\-stacks
Print folded stacks instead of listing: one line per directory, its path
from the scanned root as frames joined by
.BR ; ,
then the bytes it uses outside the directories below it.
Flame graph tools draw these lines:
.BR flamegraph.pl ,
.BR inferno\-flamegraph ,
or speedscope.
The weight follows disk use, or
.B \-\-apparent
size; other sort keys exit 2.
A
.B ;
or control character in a name prints as
.BR _ .
Works on a snapshot with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Explore one
.I PATH
a directory at a time, in the
.B \-\-sort
order (largest first by default), with a size bar per row.
Right, l or Enter opens a directory; left, h or Backspace goes up.
.B d
moves the cursor row (or the marked rows) to the trash after a
confirmation,
.B u
restores the last deletion, and
.B r
rescans the current directory.
Sizes of the parent directories follow each change without a rescan.
Each directory opens at once: its entries show while the scan runs in the
background, files with their sizes and directories with
.B …
until their totals come in, and the title counts files and bytes so far.
Moving, opening another directory and going up all work meanwhile;
trashing waits until the view has its sizes.
Given several directories
.RB ( "du \-i a b" ,
as
.B "dua i a b"
does), the explorer opens on a view of its own with one row per
directory, named by its path, and going up from one of them returns to
it; the views of the directories come with that first scan.
A directory given twice counts once, and one inside another is refused,
since it would count twice.
.B d
works on these rows too, and
.B b
needs one of the directories open.
.B q
or Ctrl-C while the current directory scans stops that scan and keeps
what it counted: the title shows
.BR (partial) ,
directories it never reached keep their placeholder rows, and
.B r
rescans in full.
Pressed again, or with no scan running, the key quits.
.BR s ", " n ", " C " and " M
order the rows by size, name, item count and newest change, in every
directory; the key of the current order reverses it.
The window title names the order.
.B S
switches sizes, bars and totals between disk use and apparent size,
and back; a size order follows the switch.
.B i
opens the details of the cursor row: full path, kind, disk use and its
share of the directory, apparent size, item count, newest change, git
state and, on Unix, mode, owner, group and link count.
Any key closes them.
.B p
quits and prints the marked paths, or the cursor row when none is
marked, on stdout once the screen is restored, one per line.
.B y
copies the same paths to the clipboard with the OSC 52 escape, which
xterm, kitty, iTerm2, WezTerm and Windows Terminal accept, and tmux with
.BR set\-clipboard " on" .
.B b
opens a shell in the current directory, as ncdu and gdu do:
.B $SHELL
or
.I /bin/sh
(on Windows
.B %COMSPEC%
or
.IR cmd.exe ).
The explorer leaves the screen while the shell runs, and Ctrl-C there
stops only the programs the shell runs.
Once the shell exits, the explorer comes back and rescans the directory.
.B v
shows the cursor file in
.B $PAGER
(split at spaces; else
.IR less ,
or
.I more
where there is no
.I less
and on Windows), and the explorer comes back when the pager exits.
.B o
opens the cursor row with its default program, as gdu does: a file in
the program that handles its type, a directory in the file manager.
It runs
.I xdg\-open
(macOS
.IR open ,
Windows
.IR explorer.exe )
on its own, so the explorer keeps the screen.
Neither key changes anything, so both work with
.BR \-\-no\-delete .
.B .
hides names that start with a dot, in this view and every view opened
after it, until pressed again; the title then says
.IR "dot names hidden" .
.B /
filters the current directory by name as you type: a part of the name,
or a glob when the text holds
.BR * ", " ? " or " [ ,
with case ignored.
Enter keeps the filter, and Esc, or
.B q
in the list, clears it.
Marking all, trashing and the title count cover only the rows shown,
and opening or leaving the directory ends the filter.
Rows show the artefact kind and git state; risky git state shows
.BR ! .
Trash directories stay closed.
Needs a terminal on stdin and the
.B tui
feature.
On Windows,
.B d
uses the Recycle Bin and
.B u
takes the newest item with that path back out.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-delete
With
.BR \-i ,
browse only:
.BR d ,
.B u
and
.B b
show a note instead of acting, and the title reads
.BR "(read\-only)" .
Safe on a shared server or a tree you only want to measure.
Without
.B \-i
it exits 2, since no other mode deletes.
The
.B no_delete
config key turns it on for every
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B \-\-si
Show sizes in powers of 1000 (kB, MB, GB, TB) instead of 1024 (KiB,
MiB, GiB), in the listing, the totals, the trash line and the explorer.
JSON, snapshots and stacks stay in bytes.
The
.B si
config key makes it the default.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS restore
.B rtrash restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIPATH\fR]
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-restore .
.PP
Restore a trashed item to its original location. With
.IR PATH ,
restore the item originally at
.IR PATH
(exact original-path match).
With
.BI \-\-name= NAME ,
restore the item whose file name, or whose name inside the trash, is
.IR NAME .
Without
.IR PATH ,
on a TTY open the interactive restore browser (filter, navigate, restore),
on Windows as well, where it shows the Recycle Bin;
without a TTY (or with
.BR \-\-plain )
print a numbered list and read a selection from stdin: indices, ranges
and commas such as
.BR 0\-2,5 ,
or
.BR all .
Pass
.B \-\-cwd\-only
to limit bare restore to originals under the current directory.
A single match restores directly; multiple matches require a selection.
.PP
Whatever the flags, restore refuses a destination that is, contains or
sits inside the item's trash or the home trash.
.B put
never trashes such a path, so only a forged or corrupt
.I .trashinfo
names one, and an overwrite there would replace the trash or a real item
in it.
A record with a relative or empty
.B Path=
outside a volume trash names no place to restore to, so restore refuses
it;
.BI \-\-to= DIR
restores such an item into
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing file at the destination (same as
.BR \-\-conflict=overwrite ).
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict= POLICY
What to do when the destination exists:
.B refuse
(the default; the item stays in the trash and the run fails),
.BR overwrite ,
.B rename
(restore next to it as
.IR STEM .1. EXT ,
counting up)
or
.B skip
(the item stays in the trash; the run still succeeds).
.TP
.BI \-\-to= DIR
Restore into the existing directory
.I DIR
instead of the original location, keeping each item's file name.
.TP
.B \-\-cwd\-only
Without PATH, only items trashed from under the current directory.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Force numbered line selection even on a TTY (skip the TUI browser).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-space\-check
Copy across devices even when the copy would leave the target file
system with under 5% of its bytes or inodes free, or under
.B RTRASH_MIN_FREE
bytes.
On Windows an item moves back within its drive, but one whose original
path is on another drive than its Recycle Bin is copied, so the same
byte rules apply to that drive before anything is restored.
.TP
.B \-\-newest\-first
List the most recently trashed item first (default in the interactive browser).
.TP
.B \-\-oldest\-first
Order from oldest to newest (default for the numbered list, so the newest row prints just above the prompt).
.TP
.BI \-\-name= NAME
Match items by original file name or by name inside the trash, instead of by
.IR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-glob= PATTERN
Match items by shell pattern: against the original path when
.I PATTERN
contains a slash, else against the original file name or the name inside
the trash.
.TP
.BI \-\-regex= REGEX
Match items whose original path contains a match for
.IR REGEX .
Give only one of
.BR \-\-name ,
.B \-\-glob
and
.BR \-\-regex .
.TP
.B \-\-latest
When several items match, restore the most recently trashed one without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-all
When several items match, restore every one without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.B {"restored": [...], "failed": N, "skipped": N}
on stdout; each restored item also names its
.IR restored_to . Implies
.BR \-\-plain ;
the numbered table, if any, goes to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS undo
.B rtrash undo
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIBATCH\fR]
.PP
Restore every item one put run trashed, each to its original location.
Each put run writes one batch id into its
.B .trashinfo
files, under the extra key
.BR X-Rtrash-Batch .
Without
.IR BATCH ,
undo the most recent run in scope.
Items trashed by other tools carry no batch id and never match.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List put runs, newest first: id, date, item count and paths.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would come back and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite existing files at the original locations (same as
.BR \-\-conflict=overwrite ).
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict= POLICY
What to do when an original location is taken, as in restore:
.B refuse
(the default; the item stays in the trash and the run fails),
.BR overwrite ,
.B rename
(restore next to it as
.IR STEM .1. EXT ,
counting up)
or
.B skip
(the item stays in the trash; the run still succeeds).
With
.BR \-n ,
each line says what the policy would do.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.RI { batch ", " restored ", " failed ", " skipped }
as JSON on stdout, each restored item with its
.BR restored_to ;
with
.BR \-\-list ,
an array of runs.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Exit status 0 means every item came back; 1 means nothing matched or an item failed.
.SS who
.B rtrash who
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.I PATH
...
.PP
List the live processes that hold
.I PATH
or anything below it, one line each: the process id, its command, how it
holds the path and the path held.
The ways are
.BI "fd " N
(an open descriptor),
.B mmap
(a mapped file),
.BR cwd ,
.B root
and
.BR exe .
Linux reads
.IR /proc/PID/fd ,
.IR cwd ,
.IR root ,
.I exe
and
.I maps
as
.BR lsof (8)
and
.BR fuser (1)
do.
macOS asks libproc for open files, working and root directories and the
executable, but not memory maps.
Without root, other users' processes cannot be read, and a note on
stderr counts them.
Windows asks the Restart Manager which processes have a file open,
checking at most 4096 files below each
.IR PATH ;
it does not see working directories, and links are not followed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON object:
.I holders
(each with
.IR pid ", " uid ", " command ", " how ", " path )
and
.I unreadable
(the count of processes that could not be read).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Exit status is 0 when some process holds a
.IR PATH ,
1 when none does or the platform cannot tell, and 2 on a usage error.
.SS reap
.B rtrash reap
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIDIR\fR]...
.PP
Trash the stale entries in temporary directories that no live process
holds.
Without
.IR DIR ,
reap looks in
.B $TMPDIR
(when set), then
.I /tmp
and
.IR /var/tmp .
It refuses
.I /
and your home directory.
An entry is a name directly inside
.IR DIR ,
taken whole.
Its age comes from the newest time anywhere in it.
Like
.BR systemd\-tmpfiles (8)
that is the newest access, change or modification time, except that a
directory's access time never counts, since listing it updates it.
.PP
An entry stays when
.IP \[u2022] 2
a live process has it or anything in it open, mapped, or as its working,
root or executable path, as
.B rtrash who
reports it;
.IP \[u2022] 2
it is or holds a socket, named pipe or device;
.IP \[u2022] 2
it is or holds a mount point;
.IP \[u2022] 2
another user owns any of it, unless reap runs as root;
.IP \[u2022] 2
it is a lock or pid file (\fI*.lock\fR, \fI*.pid\fR, \fI*\-lock\fR,
\fI*.lck\fR, at most 64 bytes) whose first word is a live process id;
.IP \[u2022] 2
an
.B x
or
.B X
line in
.IR /etc/tmpfiles.d ,
.I /run/tmpfiles.d
or
.I /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d
names it or a path inside it (specifiers such as
.B %b
match anything);
.IP \[u2022] 2
its name starts with
.IR .Trash ,
or it is or holds
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ;
.IP \[u2022] 2
a part of it cannot be read.
.PP
Processes are read once, before anything moves.
On macOS memory maps are not read.
On Windows open files come from the Restart Manager when each entry is
checked, owners and mount points are not checked, and symbolic links and
junctions are kept.
Without root, other users' processes cannot be read, but their files are
kept for being theirs.
.PP
.B Profiles.
With
.BI \-\-profile= NAME
reap also takes a tool's caches and temp files.
A profile is a list of path globs, an age and an action.
In a glob,
.I \(ti
is your home,
.B $VAR
and
.B ${VAR:\-default}
read the environment (a path whose variable is unset and has no default
is dropped),
.I C
at the start is the cache root
(\fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME\fR or \fI\(ti/.cache\fR; \fI\(ti/Library/Caches\fR on
macOS, \fI%LOCALAPPDATA%\fR on Windows), and a
.BR linux: ,
.BR macos: ,
.B unix:
or
.B windows:
prefix limits the glob to that system.
On Windows a glob may start with a drive such as
.IR C:\e ,
and the built-in profiles also cover
.IR %TEMP% ,
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%
caches and Cursor's caches under
.IR %APPDATA% .
A
.B *
never matches a leading dot, and
.B **
spans up to four directories, never entering hidden directories,
symlinks,
.I node_modules
or a match.
Each match is one entry, judged by every rule above, with the profile's
age unless
.B \-\-older\-than
is given.
.PP
The built-in profiles name only what each tool rebuilds, never its
settings, credentials, history or sessions:
.TP
.B ai
(group)
.BR claude " (" \(ti/.claude/shell\-snapshots ", " \(ti/.claude/todos ,
\fI$TMPDIR/claude\-*\fR; 7 days, trash),
.B cursor
(the Electron
.IR Cache ", " CachedData ", " "Code Cache" ", " GPUCache " and " logs
of Cursor, and
\fI\(ti/.cursor/projects/*/terminals\fR; 7 days, trash),
.B codex
(\fI$CODEX_HOME/log\fR; 14 days, trash),
.B aider
(\fI\(ti/.aider/caches\fR; 14 days, trash),
.B ollama
(runner scratch in \fI$TMPDIR/ollama*\fR, never models; 2 days, permanent).
The first four are guarded: an entry that is or holds a file named like
settings, history, a credential, a key or a database stays.
.TP
.B compilers
(group)
.B cargo\-target
(directories named
.I target
holding
.I .rustc_info.json
up to four levels below home, and
.BR $CARGO_TARGET_DIR ;
one also stays while a process named cargo or rustc holds anything in
its project, by its command name, the file name of its executable or
that of its first argument; 14 days),
.B cargo\-registry
(\fIregistry/cache\fR, \fIregistry/src\fR and \fIgit/checkouts\fR under
\fI$CARGO_HOME\fR; 30 days),
.B sccache
(\fI$SCCACHE_DIR\fR),
.B ccache
(the hex buckets of \fI$CCACHE_DIR\fR, never \fIccache.conf\fR),
.B go
(the hex buckets of \fI$GOCACHE\fR, not the module cache; 10 days),
.B zig
(the global \fIo\fR and \fItmp\fR caches),
.B bazel
(output bases no server holds),
.B gradle
(build cache and daemon logs); all permanent.
.TP
.B pkg
(group)
.B pip
(HTTP and wheel cache),
.B uv
(cache buckets),
.B npm
(\fI_cacache\fR content, \fI_logs\fR, \fI_npx\fR),
.B pnpm
(metadata cache; the store is left to
.BR "pnpm store prune" ),
.B yarn
(v1 and berry caches),
.B bun
(install cache),
.B conda
(package tarballs only, never the extracted packages environments link
to); 30 days, permanent.
.TP
.B thumbnails
(freedesktop thumbnails; 90 days, permanent).
.PP
.B \-\-list\-profiles
shows them all, and with
.B \-v
the paths each expands to here.
A profile in the config file under
.BI [reap.profiles. NAME ]
replaces the built-in of that name or adds a new one, with the keys
.B about
(a string),
.B paths
(an array of globs),
.B age
(such as
.BR \(dq7d\(dq ;
30 days without it),
.B action
.RB ( \(dqtrash\(dq ", the default, or " \(dqpermanent\(dq ),
.B include
(other profiles to run),
.B marker
(a file an entry must hold),
.B busy
(command or executable names that keep an entry while they hold
anything in its parent),
.B guard
(true to keep entries holding credential-like names),
.B roots
(directories for its
.B {roots}
globs),
.B depth
(how far
.B **
reaches) and
.B tool
(the tool's own cleanup command, where
.B {age}
becomes the age as
.I N\fBd\fR).
For example:
.PP
.nf
[reap.profiles.scratch]
about = "my scratch builds"
paths = ["\(ti/scratch/*", "linux:/var/tmp/$USER\-*"]
age = "3d"
action = "permanent"
.fi
.PP
With
.BR \-\-tool ,
a profile with a tool on PATH runs it instead of its own pass:
.BR "ccache \-\-evict\-older\-than" ,
.BR "go clean \-cache" ,
.BR "pip cache purge" ,
.BR "uv cache prune" ,
.BR "npm cache clean \-\-force" ,
.BR "pnpm store prune" ,
.BR "yarn cache clean" ,
.B bun pm cache rm
and
.BR "conda clean \-\-tarballs" .
A dry run only names the command.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile= NAME
Also reap profile
.I NAME
(repeatable).
With a profile and no
.IR DIR ,
reap leaves the temp directories alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-roots= DIR\fR[\fP:\fIDIR\fR]...
Directories that a glob starting with
.B {roots}
searches, such as
.I /workspace
for the
.B cargo\-target
profile's
.BR {roots}/**/target .
It is repeatable and takes a path list like
.BR PATH ,
and adds to
.B RTRASH_REAP_ROOTS
and the profile's
.B roots
key.
Without roots, a
.B {roots}
glob matches nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-depth= N
How many directories
.B **
spans, below home or a root; 4 by default, at most 16.
It beats the profile's
.B depth
key.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-profiles
List the profiles: name, age, action and what it covers, or
.B group
for a group.
.TP
.B \-\-tool
Hand each chosen profile that has one to its tool's own cleanup.
.TP
.B \-\-trash
Move entries to the trash even for a permanent profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than= AGE
Only entries whose newest time is older than
.IR AGE :
a number with
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d
or
.BR w ,
or days without a unit.
For DIR the default is the age column of the
.BR tmpfiles.d (5)
line
.RB ( d ", " D ", " e ", " q ", " Q
or
.BR v )
for that directory, read from
.IR /etc ", " /run " and " /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d
as systemd\-tmpfiles reads them, so
.I /var/tmp
usually waits 30 days; without such a line it is 10d.
Directories with different ages are judged separately.
.TP
.BI \-\-age\-by= LETTERS
Which times count, from
.B a
(access),
.B c
(change) and
.B m
(modification); the default is
.BR acm .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would go, and with profiles each profile's entry count and
total size, and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-permanent
Delete entries instead of moving them to the trash, for every profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Put into this trash root instead of the one put would pick.
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Always use the home trash.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also print each entry kept and why.
.TP
.BR \-\-install\-timer [=\fIEVERY\fR]
Write a per\-user job that runs this reap, with every other option
given, each
.I EVERY
(an age such as
.BR 30m ;
1h by default, at least 1m), and print the command that turns it on.
On Linux it writes
.I rtrash\-reap.service
and
.I rtrash\-reap.timer
to
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user ;
turn them on with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user daemon\-reload && systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now rtrash\-reap.timer" .
On macOS it writes
.I \(ti/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.haozeke.rtrash\-reap.plist
for
.BR "launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id \-u)" .
Neither needs root, and the job runs at low priority.
With
.B \-n
it prints the files instead of writing them.
A cheap job pairs it with
.BR \-\-when\-below ,
for example
.BR "rtrash reap \-\-install\-timer=15m \-p compilers \-p pkg \-\-when\-below=10%" .
Remove the files to remove the job.
.TP
.BI \-\-when\-below= SPEC
Reap only when a file system holding the entries has less free than
.IR SPEC :
a size such as
.BR 20G ,
a percent of its size such as
.BR 10% ,
or both, each optionally with
.BI ,inodes= PCT\fR.
While every such file system is above it, reap exits 0 at once,
without the process scan, so a timer can run it often.
Below it, entries on that file system go oldest first, profile by
profile in the order given.
Before each one, reap measures the file system again and stops once it
is back above the target; the rest are kept as
.RI \(oq "free space is back above the target" \(cq.
A trashed entry frees space only once the trash is emptied, so its size
counts toward the target as if freed; profiles whose action is
permanent, or
.BR \-\-permanent ,
suit it best.
A dry run cannot measure, so it adds up the entries' sizes instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-until= SPEC
The target to reap back to under
.BR \-\-when\-below ,
in the same form; by default the
.B \-\-when\-below
target itself.
.TP
.B \-\-shared
Also give what a delete would free, measured before anything goes: each
entry's bytes in extents no other file shares, then the extents that
reflinks or snapshots share, counted once across all entries.
These go only when no file outside the entries still uses them, so the
total frees at least the first figure and at most both.
Extents are read as for
.BR "du \-\-unique" ;
JSON entries gain
.I unique
and the object gains
.I unique
and
.IR shared .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON object:
.IR dry_run ,
.I older_than
(seconds),
.I profiles
(each with
.IR name ", " action ", " older_than ", " entries ", " bytes ", " kept ", " tool
and
.IR tool_status ),
.I reaped
(each with
.IR path ", " profile ,
.I age
in seconds and
.IR bytes ),
.I skipped
(each with
.IR path ", " profile
and
.IR reason )
and
.I failed
(the count of entries that could not be moved).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
The exit status is 0 when every stale entry went, 1 when one could not
or a
.I DIR
could not be read, and 2 on a usage error.
.SS finder
.B rtrash finder put
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B rtrash finder list
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-trash\-dir=\fIDIR\fR]
.br
.B rtrash finder restore
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fINAME\fR|\fIORIGINAL\fR...
.PP
Use the macOS Finder Trash, with Put Back.
.B put
asks Finder (through
.BR osascript )
to move each
.I FILE
to the Trash, so Finder records the original location and its own Put
Back works; macOS only, and symbolic links get refused.
.B list
prints each item as
.RB \(oq "NAME \-> ORIGINAL\-PATH" \(cq,
reading Finder's Put Back records
.RI ( ptbL
and
.IR ptbN )
from the trash's
.IR .DS_Store ,
with
.B ?
for an item without one.
.B restore
moves items back, matched by their name inside the trash or by original
path, and leaves
.I .DS_Store
for Finder to tidy.
List and restore read files only, so they also run on Linux against a
copied trash.
Experimental: tested against crafted
.I .DS_Store
files, not yet against Finder.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir= DIR
Use this Finder trash (default
.IR ~/.Trash ).
For a volume trash such as
.IR /Volumes/X/.Trashes/501 ,
Put Back paths count from the volume root above
.IR .Trashes .
.TP
.B \-\-all
Restore every item that has a Put Back record.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Put: ignore missing files.
Restore: overwrite an existing destination (same as
.BR \-\-conflict=overwrite ).
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict= POLICY
.BR refuse " (default), " overwrite ,
.B rename
or
.BR skip ,
as for
.BR restore .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Put: name each trashed file.
.TP
.B \-\-json
List: a JSON array of
.RI { name ", " original }.
Restore:
.BR {"restored": [...], "failed": N, "skipped": N} .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.SS config
.B rtrash config
[\fB\-\-path\fR | \fB\-\-default\fR]
.PP
Show the config file and the settings it makes, with a warning for each
line that cannot be read; the exit status is 1 when there is one.
Settings rank below environment variables and command-line options.
The file holds TOML tables:
.RS
.nf
[du]
top = 20                  # \-\-top
sort = "disk"             # \-\-sort: disk, apparent, items or mtime
count = false             # \-\-count
one_file_system = false   # \-x
threads = 8               # \-\-threads
git = true                # false = \-\-no\-git
exclude = ["*.iso"]       # \-\-exclude, before any given on the command line
exclude_caches = false    # \-\-exclude\-caches
gitignore = false         # \-\-gitignore
no_delete = false         # \-\-no\-delete for every \-i
si = false                # \-\-si

[list]
sort = "date"             # \-\-sort: date, size, name or path
size = false              # \-\-size
reverse = false           # \-\-reverse

[policy]
max_size = "50G"          # RTRASH_MAX_SIZE
min_free = "10G"          # RTRASH_MIN_FREE
in_use = "warn"           # RTRASH_IN_USE: warn, refuse or ignore
prune = "oldest"          # RTRASH_PRUNE: oldest or largest

[protect]
paths = ["/srv/data", "/home/*/keep"]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only these keys are read: strings, integers, booleans and arrays of
strings. An unknown key or a value of the wrong type gets a warning and
is skipped; the rest of the file still applies.
.B protect.paths
works like the safe-rm lists:
.B put
refuses a path that matches an entry exactly or as a shell pattern.
A config file cannot be undone by an option, so
.B RTRASH_CONFIG=
(empty) runs without it.
.TP
.B \-\-path
Print where the config file is looked for.
.TP
.B \-\-default
Print a config file with every setting commented out.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SS setup
.B rtrash setup
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.PP
Install multi-call symlinks, bash/zsh completions, and this man page under a
user prefix (default
.IR ~/.local ,
or
.B $PREFIX
if set). Assets are
.B embedded in the binary
so a
.B cargo install
user does not need a source checkout. Preferred post-install step:
.BR "rtrash setup" .
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix =DIR
Install root (default:
.IR ~/.local ).
.TP
.BI \-\-bin\-dir =DIR
Override the directory for multi-call symlinks (default:
.IR PREFIX/bin ).
.TP
.B \-\-with\-rm
Also link
.B rm
→ rtrash (put into trash).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without writing.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Replace existing links/files (use after upgrades).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each path written.
.SS completions
.B rtrash completions
.RB { bash | zsh }
.PP
Print the embedded completion script to stdout (packagers / custom paths).
.SS man
.B rtrash man
.PP
Print the embedded man page source to stdout.
.SS rm (permanent delete from trash)
.B rtrash rm
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.I PATTERN
...
.PP
Also multi-call
.BR trash-rm .
.PP
Permanently remove trashed items whose original path or basename matches
a
.I PATTERN
(shell-style glob; quote globs from the shell). Matching entries are
deleted from the trash
(\fBfiles/\fR and
.BR .trashinfo ),
not restored.
An orphan (no
.BR .trashinfo )
matches by its name in
.B files/
or its path there.
An absolute
.I PATTERN
also matches with symlinks resolved in its directory part (up to the
first glob), the form
.B put
records, so a path typed through a symlinked directory still matches.
.PP
Note: multi-call name
.B rm
means
.I put into trash
(safe). This command (subcommand
.B rtrash rm
or multi-call
.BR trash-rm )
permanently deletes matching
.I trash
entries — not a synonym for put.
.TP
.BI \-\-trash\-dir =PATH
Only consider this trash directory (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-home\-only
Only the home trash (skip volume trash).
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than =DAYS
Only match items trashed more than
.I DAYS
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer\-than =DAYS
Only match items trashed within the last
.I DAYS
days.
.TP
.BI \-\-larger\-than =SIZE
Only match items taking more than
.I SIZE
on disk, payload plus
.IR .trashinfo ,
the size
.B list \-\-size
shows.
.I SIZE
reads as in
.BR "empty \-\-max\-size" :
500M, 1.5G, 64k.
Payloads with no
.I .trashinfo
have a size, so this filter keeps them in.
.TP
.BI \-\-smaller\-than =SIZE
Only match items taking less than
.I SIZE
on disk.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List matches and reclaimable size; do not delete.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Allow mass patterns that match everything (e.g.
.RB \(oq * \(cq).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each permanently removed original path.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON summary with a
.B matches
array.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.PP
Examples:
.nf
.RS
rtrash rm \(aq*.o\(aq
rtrash rm \-n \(aq*.o\(aq
trash-rm foo
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base for the home trash directory
(\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR). Defaults under
.I ~/.local/share
when unset.
.TP
.B HOME
Used when resolving the default data home.
.TP
.B RTRASH_CONFIG
The config file to read instead of
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rtrash/config.toml .
Empty turns the config file off.
.TP
.B RTRASH_MAX_SIZE
When set to a size such as 10G, each put afterwards prunes every trash
directory it used back under that size, removing older items first.
Items of the same put run never go. It overrides
.B policy.max_size
in the config file, and an empty value turns the prune off.
.TP
.B RTRASH_MIN_FREE
A size such as 10G that a put or restore copying across devices must
leave free on the target file system, on top of 5% of its bytes and
inodes.
It overrides
.B policy.min_free
in the config file.
.TP
.B RTRASH_EXTENT_MIN
The smallest size on disk, such as 16K, of a file whose extents
.BR "du \-\-unique" ,
.B du \-\-shared
and
.B reap \-\-shared
map; smaller files count as unique.
The default is 64K.
.TP
.B RTRASH_REAP_ROOTS
A path list like
.B PATH
of directories that the
.B {roots}
globs of reap profiles search, after
.BR \-\-roots .
.TP
.B RTRASH_IN_USE
.BR warn ", " refuse " or " ignore :
what put does with an item a live process holds.
It overrides
.B policy.in_use
and yields to
.BR \-\-in\-use .
.TP
.B RTRASH_PRUNE
Set to
.B largest
to make that prune remove the largest items first (default
.BR oldest ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
Home trash:
.BR files/ ,
.BR info/ ,
.BR directorysizes ,
and optional lock file.
Cache sizes count allocated blocks as
.B du \-B1
does.
.B status
and
.B "empty \-\-dry\-run"
write walked sizes back.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rtrash/config.toml
Config file (see
.BR config );
.I ~/.config/rtrash/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, and
.I %APPDATA%\(rsrtrash\(rsconfig.toml
on Windows.
.TP
.IR /etc/safe\-rm.conf ", " ~/.safe\-rm
safe-rm protected paths that put skips (see
.BR put ).
.TP
.IR $top /.Trash/ $uid
or
.IR $top /.Trash\- $uid
Per-volume trash on other mounts (see the Trash specification).
A
.I $top/.Trash
that exists as a symlink or a plain file, or lacks the sticky bit,
gets a warning, and put falls back to
.IR $top /.Trash\- $uid .
Either per-user directory must be a real directory owned by the user;
one that is a symlink or belongs to someone else gets a warning, put
falls back to the next choice and in the end the home trash, and list,
empty and rm skip it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure (e.g. could not trash or purge an item).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 130
.B du
stopped by Ctrl-C.
The text listing and
.B \-\-json
still show what was scanned, marked partial;
.BR \-\-print0 ,
.BR \-\-pick ,
.B \-\-output
and
.B \-\-stacks
print nothing, since a partial list would pass for a whole one.
A second Ctrl-C ends the process at once.
.PP
Put mode mirrors common
.BR rm (1)
exit conventions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
Trash specification:
.UR https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash-spec/trashspec-latest.html
.UE
.PP
Shell completions ship in the source tree under
.I completions/
(bash and zsh). See the project README for install paths.
